.TH cusync n 0.3 ctrlutils
.SH ctrlutils::cusync
.PP
A controller that compares two directory trees and mirrors one onto the other. Each tree lives on its own \fBtuprovider\fR, so \fBlocal <\-> ZIP <\-> SFTP <\-> WebDAV\fR combinations all work. Sync is \fBone\-way\fR and \fBnon\-destructive\fR by default: it copies missing entries and updates newer/differing ones, but it never deletes on the target. There is no Tk here \-\- the Explorer supplies the dialog and the progress bar; cusync is pure model/controller logic.
.SS API
.CS
package require ctrlutils::cusync

::ctrlutils::cusync::compare  $provL $dirL $provR $dirR        ;# -> list of diff dicts
::ctrlutils::cusync::plan     $diffs $direction               ;# -> list of action dicts
::ctrlutils::cusync::apply    $srcProv $srcDir $dstProv $dstDir $actions \
                              ?-ondir cb? ?-onfile cb?         ;# -> {done N errors {..}}
::ctrlutils::cusync::applyOne $srcProv $srcDir $dstProv $dstDir $action ;# -> {ok 0|1 ?error msg?}
.CE
.PP
The flow is always \fBcompare \-> plan \-> apply\fR: compare tells you how the two sides differ, plan turns that into a copy list for one direction, apply (or \fBapplyOne\fR in a loop) executes it.
.SS compare
.PP
\fBcompare $provL $dirL $provR $dirR\fR walks both trees and returns a flat list of diff dicts, parents before children. Each diff dict has:
.TS
l l.
Key	Meaning
\fBrel\fR	path relative to the two roots, \fB/\fR\-separated, no leading slash
\fBname\fR	leaf name
\fBstatus\fR	see below
\fBltype\fR	\fBfile\fR, \fBdir\fR, or \fB""\fR if absent on the left
\fBrtype\fR	\fBfile\fR, \fBdir\fR, or \fB""\fR if absent on the right
.TE
.PP
\fBstatus\fR is one of:
.TS
l l.
Status	Meaning
\fBonlyLeft\fR	present only on the left
\fBonlyRight\fR	present only on the right
\fBnewerLeft\fR	on both, left mtime is newer
\fBnewerRight\fR	on both, right mtime is newer
\fBdiff\fR	on both, same mtime but different size
\fBsame\fR	on both, same mtime and size
\fBconflict\fR	one side is a file, the other a directory
.TE
.PP
Left\-only directories are recursed into, so every file underneath gets its own diff entry (and can be mirrored).
.PP
\fBsame\fR compares metadata only: same size and same mtime. Two files with equal metadata and different contents count as \fBsame\fR; cusync reads no contents.
.PP
A directory that exists on a side but cannot be listed raises \fB{CTRLUTILS CUSYNC LIST side dir}\fR (\fBside\fR is \fBL\fR or \fBR\fR). Up to 0.2 the error was caught and that side counted as empty, so a failed listing of the target turned every source file into a copy over it. A subtree that is only on one side is not listed on the other.
.SS plan
.PP
\fBplan $diffs $direction\fR turns diffs into a list of action dicts for one direction. \fBdirection\fR is \fBLR\fR (left is the source) or \fBRL\fR (right is the source). Each action dict is \fB{op copy rel <path> type file|dir}\fR.
.PP
A plan copies, in the chosen direction, the entries that are \fBmissing\fR on the target plus the ones that are \fBnewer or differing\fR on the source. It never emits a delete and never pulls the target\-only side across \-\- an \fBLR\fR plan ignores \fBonlyRight\fR, an \fBRL\fR plan ignores \fBonlyLeft\fR. That is what makes sync non\-destructive: run it and the target only ever gains or refreshes files.
.SS "apply / applyOne"
.PP
\fBapply $srcProv $srcDir $dstProv $dstDir $actions\fR executes a plan: directories are created, files are read from the source and written to the target (parent directories are created as needed). It returns \fB{done N errors {msg ...}}\fR \-\- \fBdone\fR counts successful actions, \fBerrors\fR collects per\-action messages so a partial failure does not abort the whole run.
.PP
Optional \fB\-ondir\fR / \fB\-onfile\fR callbacks receive the \fBrel\fR path before each action, for progress reporting. They are invoked at global scope; errors in a callback are ignored.
.PP
\fBapplyOne $srcProv $srcDir $dstProv $dstDir $action\fR runs a single action and returns \fB{ok 1}\fR or \fB{ok 0 error <msg>}\fR. It is factored out so a UI can step through a plan one action at a time (via \fBafter\fR), keeping the event loop live for a progress bar and a Cancel button instead of freezing. \fBapply\fR is just a loop over \fBapplyOne\fR.
.PP
For local\-to\-local copies the source mtime is preserved on a best\-effort basis so repeated mirrors are idempotent; this is silently skipped for zip/dav/sftp targets.
.SS Example
.CS
package require ctrlutils::cusync

set L [tclutils::tuprovider open local]
set R [tclutils::tuprovider open local]

set diffs [::ctrlutils::cusync::compare $L /home/me/src $R /backup/src]
set plan  [::ctrlutils::cusync::plan $diffs LR]
set res   [::ctrlutils::cusync::apply $L /home/me/src $R /backup/src $plan \
              -onfile [list puts]]
puts "copied [dict get $res done], errors: [dict get $res errors]"
.CE
.SS "See also"
.PP
\fBcufileops\fR, \fBcuundo\fR, \fBtclutils::tuprovider\fR
